ight directions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal), there is a piece of the player’s own color such that all positions between it and the new piece are occupied solely by the opponent’s pieces, with no empty spaces in between."\n\nSo "all positions between" must be opponent\'s. If there are no positions between (i.e., adjacent), then this condition is satisfied (since all zero positions are opponent\'s), but in reality, there\'s no opponent\'s piece to flip. However, according to the problem statement\'s rule 2, the move is allowed only if in some direction, there exists a own piece with all between being opponent\'s.\n\nBut in standard Othello rules, a move must flip at least one piece, so adjacent placement without sandwiching is invalid.\n\nGiven that the problem statement\'s valid actions are provided, we can assume that the listed moves are valid, so perhaps in this case, (A,7) is valid because in the south direction, B7 is @, then C7 is *.\n\nWait, for (A,7):\n\nLooking south: A7 -> B7 (@), then C7 (*). So between A7 and C7 is B7 (@), which is opponent\'s. So yes, that\'s a valid direction. So placing at A7 would flip B7. So that\'s one piece.\n\nAlso, check west direction: A7 -> A6 (.), which is empty. East is A8 (.), empty.\n\nSoutheast: A7 -> B8 (@), but B8 is @, then C9 which is invalid. So no.\n\nSouthwest: A7 -> B6 (*). B6 is *, so adjacent. So between A7 and B6 is nothing, so no opponent\'s pieces. So this direction doesn\'t contribute.\n\nSo only south direction (B7) is flipped. So 1 piece.\n\nNext, (B,3):\n\nB3. Let\'s check.\n\nRow B, column3.\n\nCheck directions.\n\nNorth: A3 (.), which is empty.\n\nSouth: C3 (.), D3 (*). So B3 -> C3 (.) -> D3 (*). But C3 is empty, so no.\n\nEast: B4 (@), B5 (@), B6 (*). So direction east: B3 -> B4 (@), B5 (@), B6 (*). So between B3 and B6 are two @s. So this would flip B4 and B5. So that\'s two pieces.\n\nAlso, check other directions.\n\nNortheast: B3 -> A4 (.). Empty.\n\nSoutheast: B3 -> C4 (.). Row C, column4 is